1
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:13.830
Everything here follows the same four
steps: download, fill, upload, confirm.

2
00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:22.082
One tab per record type. We'll do tenants
first — the flow is identical everywhere.

3
00:00:23.212 --> 00:00:30.212
The template is pre-formatted. Never build
your own spreadsheet from scratch.

4
00:00:30.226 --> 00:00:36.643
Row two tells you which columns are
required. The Instructions sheet explains

5
00:00:36.643 --> 00:00:38.143
every column.

6
00:00:38.143 --> 00:00:46.024
Upload it. Nothing is imported yet — the
rows are checked in your browser first.

7
00:00:47.171 --> 00:00:54.600
One row is missing its rent, and the app
says so — per row, before anything is

8
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.100
saved.

9
00:00:56.341 --> 00:01:02.341
Import the valid rows. The broken one just
waits for a fix — no all-or-nothing.

10
00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:07.363
Three imported. And here's the safety net:
run the same file again…

11
00:01:11.498 --> 00:01:17.998
…and everything is skipped. Duplicates are
detected, not doubled.

12
00:01:20.257 --> 00:01:26.953
Now the power move. Expenses can export
your live data, pre-filled into the

13
00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.453
template.

14
00:01:28.453 --> 00:01:34.892
See the ID column? That's what lets you
edit anything in Excel without creating a

15
00:01:34.892 --> 00:01:36.392
duplicate.

16
00:01:36.392 --> 00:01:43.785
Tick "Update existing records" — matched
rows are refreshed instead of skipped.

17
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:49.300
Every edit landed on the right row. No
duplicates, no re-keying.

18
00:01:50.448 --> 00:01:56.566
Same pattern for units, income, petty cash
and the rest. Your spreadsheet era is

19
00:01:56.566 --> 00:01:58.066
over.

20
00:01:58.066 --> 00:02:00.963
Next: how invoices and income stay in sync
— so you never book rent twice.
